     buyingGuide: {
-      title: 'Poster Buying Guide',
+      title: 'The Complete Poster Printing Guide',
       paragraphs: [
-        'When choosing poster size, consider viewing distance and display space. A2 suits close viewing (in-store decor, display boards). A1 is the most common exhibition size, balancing visual impact and space requirements. A0 suits long-distance viewing and large spaces (conference backdrops, mall atriums). For extra-large outdoor ads, mesh material with seam output is recommended.',
-        'Material choice determines poster lifespan and effect. Short-term indoor use (exhibitions, events) suits art paper or PP synthetic paper — economical with vibrant colors. Long-term indoor display suits PP synthetic paper or mounted boards that resist curling. Outdoor use must choose PVC or canvas with waterproof and UV-resistant properties for 2–3 year fade resistance.',
-        'Resolution settings are often overlooked. Large posters (A0+) are viewed from distance — 72–100dpi is sufficiently clear. Excessively high resolution increases file size and processing time without improving actual visual effect. Small posters (A2) viewed at close range should use 150dpi to ensure crisp detail.',
-        'Shopping mall atriums, transit corridors, and shop windows often generate searches for "poster printing," "A1 poster," and "waterproof poster." If displaying in semi-outdoor or humid environments (such as near outlying island ferries), prioritize PP / PVC and evaluate mounting options to avoid paper edge curling from moisture.',
+        'Poster printing outputs visual designs at large format for display at specific locations. Unlike flyers, which are handed to individuals, posters are read at distance and in motion — pedestrians typically scan them from three to five meters away within one to two seconds. This creates two physical constraints: the material must obey the light, moisture and temperature of the display environment; and the font size plus color contrast must obey the viewing distance. At ZprintPro we grade posters into five environment tiers — indoor short-term, indoor long-term, semi-outdoor, outdoor short-term and outdoor long-term — each mapped to a distinct material combination.',
+        'The first divide in material selection is water. Pure indoor short-term displays (promotions and event posters under two weeks) run perfectly on ordinary 128-200gsm coated paper — cheapest and most vivid. Indoor long-term (over a month) fights dust, touching and daylight, so choose 200gsm coated with lamination, or switch straight to PP synthetic — waterproof and scratch-resistant, leaving no fingerprints. Semi-outdoor spots (arcades, corridors, sheltered entrances) catch rain splash, making laminated PP synthetic or PVC adhesive the minimum.',
+        'True outdoor is another world: sun, rain and wind make paper completely unsuitable. For outdoor short-term (under a month), use 440g PVC banner or vehicle-grade adhesive. For outdoor long-term (over three months), you need vehicle-grade PVC with anti-UV lamination plus outdoor pigment inks to push the fade horizon to a three-year scale. Many customers assume "lamination equals waterproof" — ordinary film only resists splashes; long-term outdoor is a system of ink plus film. For lightboxes, always specify dedicated backlit film: ordinary paper shows hot spots under backlight.',
+        'Size is reverse-engineered from viewing distance. Half a meter to one meter (elevators, beside counters, menu boards): A3 minimum, 12pt type. One to three meters (mall corridors, restaurant exteriors, expo booths): A2 or 500×700mm minimum, 24pt. Three to eight meters (subway passages, mall atriums): A1 or 600×900mm, 48pt. Eight to twenty meters (lightboxes, large hanging banners): A0 or 1200×1800mm, 96pt. Beyond twenty meters — hoarding and building wraps — you enter custom sizes with 200pt-plus headline type. A practical test: scale the artwork to one percent of the real viewing distance, step back, and if you can read the headline, it passes.',
+        'Mounting must match the material: tape or Blu-Tack only for indoor paper posters under 200gsm; self-adhesive backing suits glass, metal and smooth walls; hanging banners need punched grommets after lamination, or the paper tears from the hole; long-term outdoor belongs in aluminum stretch frames, where the frame is reusable and only the print is replaced. On color, large format fails more visibly than small: RGB-to-CMYK gamut loss dulls vivid screen colors, large solid fields show ink mottling, and multi-panel jobs printed in separate batches shift in tone. Our fixed practice — same order, same machine, one continuous run; darken solid fields slightly; CMYK preview before printing — eliminates ninety percent of large-format color problems.',
+      ],
+      links: [
+        { label: 'Flyer Printing (back to hub: hand-distribution collateral)', href: '/en/category/flyers/' },
+        { label: 'Folded Leaflet Printing (sibling: multi-panel folds)', href: '/en/product/folded-leaflets/' },
+        { label: 'Custom Box Printing (cross-cluster: visual display collateral)', href: '/en/category/packaging/' },
       ],
     },
     faq: [
